===Etymology===
Hamp derives this from {{inh|sga|cel-pro|*sonts}}, plural {{m|cel-pro|*sontes}} (whence {{m|sga|ot}}); ultimately from {{inh|sga|ine-pro|*h₁sónts}}.<ref name="Hamp Varia">{{cite-journal| last=Hamp | first=Eric P. | title=Varia II | journal=Ériu | publisher=Royal Irish Academy | volume=29 | year=1978 | issn=2009-0056 | jstor=30007771 | pages=149–154 | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/30007771 | accessdate=August 27, 2022}}</ref> Copular origin explains the use of independent subject pronouns with this conjunction, which otherwise are usually used with the copula {{m|sga|is}}.

A more traditional theory, assumed by Pedersen and Thurneysen among others, supposes that this is a contraction of {{m|sga|ocus|t=and}}, with the apparent copular behaviour being analogical.<ref name="Castillero extraclausal">{{cite-journal| last= García Castillero | first=Carlos | title=OLD IRISH TONIC PRONOUNS AS EXTRACLAUSAL CONSTITUENTS | journal=Ériu | publisher=Royal Irish Academy | volume=63 | year=2013 | issn=20090056| jstor=42910162 | pages=1–39 | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/42910162}}</ref>

===Conjunction===
